  { id:'opmcq-c20-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:"A 65-year-old woman is scheduled for cataract surgery under retrobulbar anesthesia. During the pre-operative assessment, she mentions a severe allergic reaction with anaphylaxis after receiving lidocaine at the dentist last year. What is the most appropriate action?",
    options:["Proceed with retrobulbar block using lidocaine","Give antihistamine and continue normally","Avoid local anesthetic and choose an alternative technique","Repeat lidocaine test dose"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Avoid the local anaesthetic and choose an alternative technique.** The chapter opens on exactly this instruction: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.255 ends the section on drug administration with an N.B. in its own right — *before administering any medication, always ask patients if they may be allergic to the agent.* The pre-operative assessment in this stem is that question being asked, and it has returned a positive answer of the most severe kind. Anaphylaxis is not a caution; it is a contraindication.\n\n**What the book says about this drug class.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.258 lists **cataract surgery** among the nine uses of local anaesthetics, names the three topical agents (proparacaine 0.5%, tetracaine 0.5%, benoxinate 0.4%) and gives three adverse reactions: early stinging, corneal epithelial damage on prolonged or uncontrolled use, and **local or systemic allergic reactions (rare)**. The same page also warns that combining two or more anaesthetics **does not produce an additive effect but increases the risk of toxicity** — so \"use a bit of something else as well\" is not a route out either.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Proceed with retrobulbar block using lidocaine | ✗ Re-exposes a patient with documented anaphylaxis to the agent that caused it, and does so by **injection into the orbit** — a far larger dose and a far faster systemic uptake than a dental infiltration. |\n| Give antihistamine and continue normally | ✗ Antihistamine pre-treatment blunts urticaria and itching; it does not prevent anaphylaxis, which is mediated well beyond histamine alone. Premedicating and proceeding converts a foreseeable event into an unmonitored one. |\n| **Avoid local anesthetic and choose an alternative technique** | ✅ The only option that removes the trigger. In practice that means general anaesthesia, or a technique using an agent from a class the patient has tolerated. |\n| Repeat lidocaine test dose | ✗ A test dose in a patient with prior anaphylaxis is a deliberate challenge with the offending drug. Nothing is learned that the history has not already established, and the reaction it may provoke is the one being avoided. |\n\n⚠️ **A pharmacological nuance the bank does not test, recorded because it changes the real-world answer.** Lidocaine is an **amide**; the three topical agents this chapter names — proparacaine, tetracaine and benoxinate — are **esters**. True cross-reactivity between the amide and ester classes is not expected, so a genuine amide allergy does not by itself forbid every local anaesthetic. *(The amide/ester division and its cross-reactivity are **not taken from the course material** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.258 lists the three agents without classifying them.)* The key still stands: with anaphylaxis documented and the offending agent uncertain in a pre-operative interview, avoiding local anaesthesia and choosing an alternative technique is the safe action, and it is the one the bank rewards.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Act on a documented anaphylactic reaction to a local anaesthetic by abandoning the block and choosing an alternative anaesthetic technique, rather than re-challenging, test-dosing or premedicating.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.128 (Ch.20 Q1, the first question of the chapter, printed directly beneath the red banner "20. Drugs & the eye").' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 28-year-old woman presents with mild itching and eye redness after dust exposure. Vision is normal, there is no discharge, and no papillae or follicles are seen. The conjunctival vessels are mildly dilated but the eye is otherwise healthy. Which medication can provide temporary symptomatic relief of redness?",
    options:["Topical steroids","Topical vasoconstrictors","Topical antibiotics","Topical antivirals"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Topical vasoconstrictors (ocular decongestants).** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.261 defines the class in the terms this stem is built from: they are **weak adrenergic-stimulating drugs** that **temporarily whiten the conjunctiva through vasoconstriction**, and they are advertised for redness caused by *smoke, **dust**, smog, wind, glare, swimming, contact lenses, or fatigue*. The three named agents are **naphazoline hydrochloride 0.012%, phenylephrine hydrochloride 0.12% and tetrahydrozoline hydrochloride 0.05%**.\n\nThe stem is written to exclude every disease that would need real treatment: **normal vision** (no corneal or intraocular involvement), **no discharge** (not bacterial), **no papillae** (not allergic proper), **no follicles** (not viral or chlamydial). What is left is a mildly injected but otherwise healthy eye — an irritation, not an infection.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Topical steroids | ✗ ⚠️ The dangerous answer. p.261 states that topical ocular corticosteroids **should never be prescribed by a primary care provider**, and lists their serious complications: promotion of **viral, bacterial and fungal infections**, and possible **glaucoma and cataract**. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-793` keys the same triad. Reaching for a steroid to whiten a dusty eye is an unacceptable trade. |\n| **Topical vasoconstrictors** | ✅ Temporary symptomatic whitening by vasoconstriction — exactly what the question asks for, and the only thing this class is good for. |\n| Topical antibiotics | ✗ There is no discharge and no infection. p.262 warns that inappropriate long-term antibiotic use is a significant risk for **antibiotic-resistant organisms**, and that neomycin-containing agents themselves cause redness and tearing through allergic sensitivity. |\n| Topical antivirals | ✗ Acyclovir and its relatives treat **herpes simplex** infections and are for short-term use under an ophthalmologist's direction only (p.262). No dendrite, no follicles, no indication. |\n\n⚠️ **Read the word *temporary*.** The book is unusually blunt here: the belief that these compounds are part of good ocular hygiene is *a misconception*, and a **cold, wet compress to the closed lids** may benefit red, burning eyes as much as the drops do (p.261). Overuse produces **rebound vasodilation** — see `opmcq-c20-25` and `opmcq-c20-12`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Select a topical vasoconstrictor for temporary symptomatic relief of minor conjunctival redness, and reject steroids, antibiotics and antivirals in an eye with no sign of infection or true inflammation.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.128 (Ch.20 Q2).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is noted as +ve (Jones I) test?",
    options:["Fluorescein recovered from the nose","Fluorescein not recovered from the nose","Fluorescein-stained saline is recovered from the nose","Unstained saline is recovered from the nose","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Fluorescein recovered from the nose — the primary (Jones I) test is positive, and the lacrimal drainage system is patent.** This entry carries the **shared lacrimal-patency table** for the chapter; `opmcq-c20-4`, `opmcq-c20-8` and `opmcq-c20-20` all point back here rather than repeat it.\n\n**The full table, from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.257** (a single full-page ruled table headed *Lacrimal system evaluation*):\n\n| Test | How it is done | +ve means | -ve means |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Dye disappearance test** | Fluorescein instilled; the conjunctival sac is inspected 5 minutes later | — | Dye retention of **more than 5 minutes** indicates **inadequate lacrimal drainage** (`opmcq-c20-20`) |\n| **Primary (Jones 1)** | 5 minutes after fluorescein is instilled in the conjunctival sac, a cotton-tipped bud moistened with local anaesthetic is inserted **under the inferior turbinate** | **Fluorescein recovered from the nose** → **patency** of the drainage system | **No dye recovered** → **partial obstruction** of the lacrimal passages, or **failure of the lacrimal pump** |\n| **Secondary (Jones II)** | Performed **only if the primary test is negative**. The system is **irrigated with saline** while a bud sits under the inferior turbinate | **Fluorescein-stained saline** recovered → **partial obstruction** of the drainage system | **Unstained saline** recovered → **upper lacrimal (punctal or canalicular) dysfunction** or **lacrimal pump failure** (`opmcq-c20-4`) |\n\n**N.B. (p.257):** in **complete** obstruction, regurgitation of fluorescein-stained saline occurs through the **upper punctum**.\n\n**The logic of Jones II in one line:** if stained saline comes through, dye *had* reached the sac and the block is below it (partial, lower); if the saline comes through *clean*, the dye never got into the sac at all, so the fault is above it — punctum, canaliculus or pump.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Fluorescein recovered from the nose** | ✅ Jones I positive: the passages are patent. |\n| Fluorescein not recovered from the nose | ✗ That is Jones I **negative** — partial obstruction or pump failure, and the trigger to proceed to Jones II. |\n| Fluorescein-stained saline is recovered from the nose | ✗ Belongs to **Jones II positive**, not Jones I — no saline is irrigated in the primary test. |\n| Unstained saline is recovered from the nose | ✗ **Jones II negative** — the key to `opmcq-c20-4`. |\n| None of the above | ✗ A is correct. |\n\n**Corroboration from the lectures.** `L9) lacrimal system..txt` slide 18 (\"Jones I test / Fluoresceine drops in the conjunctival sac\") captions its three panels *\"Positive recovery in the nose = patent lacrimal system\"*, *\"Negative recovery in the nose = lower duct obstruction or Atony of the sac\"* and *\"Negative recovery in the nose = upper canalicular stenosis\"*. Slide 19 gives Jones II as *Positive → lacrimal duct obstruction / atony of the sac*; *Negative → common canalicular obstruction / canalicular stenosis*. The deck localises the block more finely than the book does, but the polarity is identical. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-804` tests the sequence directly: primary Jones negative → **perform Jones II**.\n\n⚠️ **A printed inconsistency in the source, recorded not corrected:** p.257 heads the first test **\"Primary test (Jones 1)\"** with an ARABIC numeral and the second **\"Secondary test (Jones II)\"** with a ROMAN one, on the same page. The bank follows the book, printing \"Jones I\" here and \"Jones II\" in `opmcq-c20-4`.\n\n⚠️ **Repair recorded — the spelling of the dye.** The bank prints the dye as **\"Flourescien\"** in the option menus of Q3, Q4, Q6 and Q8, but spells it correctly as **\"fluorescein\"** in Q20's stem. Both forms are on the page. All four defective occurrences per menu are repaired to **fluorescein** in the shipped options here and in `opmcq-c20-4`, `opmcq-c20-6` and `opmcq-c20-8`; this note records the inconsistency once for all four.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Interpret a positive primary (Jones I) dye test as recovery of fluorescein from the nose indicating a patent lacrimal drainage system, and place it correctly against the negative result and against both results of Jones II.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.128 (Ch.20 Q3). The three fluorescein options print as "Flourescien" in the bank; repaired here and the misspelling recorded in the explanation.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is noted as -ve (Jones II) test?",
    options:["Fluorescein recovered from the nose","Fluorescein not recovered from the nose","Fluorescein-stained saline is recovered from the nose","Unstained saline is recovered from the nose","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Unstained saline is recovered from the nose.** The secondary (Jones II) test is performed **only if the primary test is negative**: the lacrimal drainage system is irrigated with saline while a cotton-tipped bud sits under the inferior turbinate (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.257). What comes back is read for **colour**, not for volume.\n\n- **Stained** saline reaching the nose means fluorescein from the earlier instillation had already entered the sac, so the passage above the sac is open and the fault is a **partial obstruction** lower down.\n- **Unstained** saline reaching the nose means the dye never got into the sac at all. Irrigation under pressure has forced clean fluid through a system the tears themselves could not enter — so the fault lies **above** the sac: **upper lacrimal (punctal or canalicular) dysfunction, or lacrimal pump failure**. That is the negative test and the key here.\n\nThe complete lacrimal-patency table — dye disappearance, Jones I and Jones II with both polarities, plus the N.B. on complete obstruction — is set out once in `opmcq-c20-3` and is not repeated.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fluorescein recovered from the nose | ✗ **Jones I positive** (`opmcq-c20-3`). No dye is recovered as such in the secondary test; what is recovered is irrigating saline, stained or not. |\n| Fluorescein not recovered from the nose | ✗ **Jones I negative** — the finding that *triggers* Jones II rather than a Jones II result. |\n| Fluorescein-stained saline is recovered from the nose | ✗ **Jones II positive**: partial obstruction of the drainage system. This is the trap option — it is a Jones II result, but the opposite polarity to the one asked for. |\n| **Unstained saline is recovered from the nose** | ✅ Jones II negative: upper lacrimal dysfunction or lacrimal pump failure. |\n| None of the above | ✗ D is correct. |\n\n⚠️ **This question and `opmcq-c20-3` are a template pair** — the same five options in the same printed order, differing only in the test named and the polarity asked for. Read the stem twice: *which test*, and *+ve or -ve*. They are **not** duplicates and both are shipped.\n\n`L9) lacrimal system..txt` slide 19 gives the same polarity from the lecturer's side, localising more finely: Jones II **positive** → *lacrimal duct obstruction / atony of the sac*; **negative** → *common canalicular obstruction / canalicular stenosis* — i.e. negative places the lesion in the upper system, exactly as the book does.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Interpret a negative secondary (Jones II) test — unstained saline recovered from the nose — as upper lacrimal or pump failure, and distinguish it from the positive result and from both Jones I results.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.128 (Ch.20 Q4, the last question on the page; its option list is identical in text and order to Q3). The three fluorescein options print as "Flourescien" in the bank; repaired here, with the spelling inconsistency recorded in `opmcq-c20-3`.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:"Anti-VEGF medications are used in all of the following conditions except?",
    options:["Dry AMD","DME","PDR","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Dry AMD.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.263 gives the indications for anti-vascular endothelial growth factor drugs in one line: they are used in **wet AMD, diabetic macular oedema and PDR**. They work by **blocking the action of VEGF, thus preventing ocular angiogenesis and new vessel formation**, and are given by **intravitreal injection**. The agents most commonly used are **bevacizumab (off-label use), ranibizumab and aflibercept**.\n\nThe unifying feature of the three listed indications is that each is driven by **VEGF-mediated new vessel growth or leakage**. Dry (non-exudative, atrophic) AMD is not: it is a degenerative loss of retinal pigment epithelium and photoreceptors with no neovascular component, so there is no VEGF-driven process for the drug to block.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Dry AMD** | ✅ The exception. p.263 names **wet** AMD, and only wet AMD, among the indications; the dry form is not a neovascular disease. |\n| DME | ✗ Diabetic macular oedema is listed on p.263. Leakage here is VEGF-driven, and the drug reduces it. |\n| PDR | ✗ Proliferative diabetic retinopathy is listed on p.263 — new vessel formation is the defining lesion, and blocking VEGF is aimed straight at it. |\n| None of the above | ✗ Dry AMD genuinely is an exception, so a \"no exception\" answer cannot stand. |\n\nGrade Gain's `opqb-t20-818` builds the same fact into a vignette — a 72-year-old diabetic with sudden central visual loss and **wet** AMD — and `opqb-t20-819` keys the route as intravitreal.\n\n⚠️ **The distinction is therapeutic, not academic.** Wet AMD has a drug; dry AMD, in this book, does not. Reading \"AMD\" without its adjective loses the whole question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Diabetic Retinopathy.)*",
    objective:"List the three indications for anti-VEGF therapy given by the book — wet AMD, diabetic macular oedema and PDR — and exclude dry AMD as a non-neovascular disease.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.129 (Ch.20 Q5, the first question on the page).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:"Which of the following is an adverse effect of IV fluorescein administration?",
    options:["Nausea & vomiting","Yellowish discoloration of skin and urine","Hypersensitivity","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All of the above.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.258 opens a separate heading — *Systemic fluorescein* — and lists the adverse effects of **intravenous** fluorescein administration as exactly three items, which are exactly the first three options here:\n\n| Adverse effect (p.258) | Detail as printed |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Yellowish discolouration of the skin and urine** | Usually **fades within 36 hours** |\n| **Nausea, vomiting and headache** | — |\n| **Hypersensitivity (rare)** | Ranges from **itching and hives up to anaphylactic shock** |\n\nSodium fluorescein for injection comes in ampoules of **10% and 25% solutions** and is used for **ophthalmic angiography**: the dye is given intravenously and watched as it enters the blood vessels, **enabling evaluation of the integrity of the retinal vasculature** (p.258). That systemic route is what separates this question from the topical dye — p.256 states plainly that **no systemic complications accompany the use of topical fluorescein**, whose single local complication is **staining of soft contact lenses**.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Nausea & vomiting | ✗ True but incomplete — one of three. |\n| Yellowish discoloration of skin and urine | ✗ True but incomplete — one of three. |\n| Hypersensitivity | ✗ True but incomplete — one of three, and the only one that can kill. |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ The book's list is all three, so the collective option is the key. |\n| None of the above | ✗ All three are printed on p.258. |\n\n⚠️ **Cross-bank pair, not a reprint.** Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-806` asks the same question — *adverse effect of IV fluorescein* — but with a different menu (permanent retinal damage / **yellow skin/urine** / orbital cellulitis / optic neuritis) and therefore a different key. The two are a genuine topical pair across banks and are both shipped; neither folds into the other.\n\n⚠️ **Repairs recorded.** The stem prints **\"is an the adverse effect\"** — a doubled article — and spells the dye **\"IV flourescien\"**. Both are repaired in the shipped stem; the dye's spelling inconsistency across the chapter is recorded once in `opmcq-c20-3`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recall all three adverse effects of intravenous fluorescein — yellow discolouration of skin and urine, nausea and vomiting, and hypersensitivity up to anaphylaxis — and contrast them with the absence of systemic complications from topical fluorescein.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.129 (Ch.20 Q6). The stem prints "is an the adverse effect of IV flourescien administration"; the doubled article and the dye spelling are repaired here and recorded in the explanation.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding prostaglandin analogues except?",
    options:["They increase aqueous outflow through uveo-scleral pathway","They cause major systemic toxic effects","They may cause darkening of the iris","They are reported to cause anterior uveitis","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**They cause major systemic toxic effects — this is the false statement.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.266 says the opposite in as many words: **no major systemic toxic effects of these drugs have been reported**, *but* there are **unique ocular effects**. That contrast is the whole point of the class. Prostaglandin analogues are the anti-glaucoma drugs whose trouble is confined to the eye and the periocular tissues, unlike beta-blockers (`opmcq-c20-21`), alpha-2 agonists (`opmcq-c20-17`) and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (`opmcq-c20-26`), whose systemic side effects fill the rest of this chapter.\n\n**The seven unique ocular effects, from p.266:**\n\n| # | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Darkening of the iris** in patients with **light-coloured irides** |\n| 2 | **Eyelid skin hyperpigmentation** |\n| 3 | **Hypertrichosis** — lengthening and thickening of the eyelashes, usually after several months |\n| 4 | **Conjunctival hyperaemia** |\n| 5 | **Anterior uveitis** |\n| 6 | **Cystoid macular oedema (CME)**, especially after cataract surgery or after vascular disease such as CRVO |\n| 7 | **Prostaglandin-associated periorbitopathy (PAP)** → periorbital fat atrophy; **reversible on discontinuing the drug** |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| They increase aqueous outflow through uveo-scleral pathway | ✗ True — p.266 gives this as the mechanism: outflow through the uveoscleral pathway, *a supplemental route through which a small portion of aqueous normally drains*. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-825` keys the same mechanism. |\n| **They cause major systemic toxic effects** | ✅ False, and therefore the answer to an *except* question. |\n| They may cause darkening of the iris | ✗ True — effect 1, and the one Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-826` calls the side effect *unique* to latanoprost. |\n| They are reported to cause anterior uveitis | ✗ True — effect 5. |\n| None of the above | ✗ Option B is false, so an exception exists. |\n\n⚠️ **Read both pages before trusting a summary.** The full-page summary table on p.268 lists the same seven ocular effects but **drops the \"no major systemic toxic effects\" line entirely**, along with PAP's reversibility. A reader who works only from that table has no printed basis for answering this question. The statement the key turns on is on **p.266** and nowhere else.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State that prostaglandin analogues have no major systemic toxic effects, and list their unique ocular effects including iris darkening and anterior uveitis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.129 (Ch.20 Q7).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is among the uses of fluorescein dye?",
    options:["Check stability of precorneal tear film","Test patency of lacrimal drainage system","A & B","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A & B — both.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.256 lists **lacrimal system evaluation** as the fourth topical indication for sodium fluorescein and defers the detail to p.257, where the whole page is one table headed *Lacrimal system evaluation*. That table divides the use into exactly the two halves this question offers:\n\n| Half of the table (p.257) | Test | Threshold |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **a. Check the stability of the precorneal tear film** | **Tear film break-up time (TBUT)** — the interval between the last complete blink and the appearance of the first randomly distributed dark spot on the corneal surface | **Less than 10 seconds** indicates **dry eye** |\n| **b. Test the patency of the lacrimal drainage system** | Dye disappearance test, then **Jones I** and, if that is negative, **Jones II** | Dye retention **more than 5 minutes** indicates inadequate drainage (`opmcq-c20-20`) |\n\nBecause the book's own heading splits the same indication into (a) and (b), an option offering only one of them is a half-answer; the collective option is the key. The full patency table is in `opmcq-c20-3`.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Check stability of precorneal tear film | ✗ True but incomplete — half (a) alone. |\n| Test patency of lacrimal drainage system | ✗ True but incomplete — half (b) alone. |\n| **A & B** | ✅ Both are printed uses, under one heading on one page. |\n| Neither A nor B | ✗ Both are uses. |\n\n⚠️ **These are not the dye's only uses.** p.256 lists **four** topical indications: assessment of **ocular surface integrity** (punctate erosions, abrasions, ulceration staining green; leaking corneal wounds shown by the **Seidel test**, `Figure (20-1)`), **contact lens fitting**, **applanation tonometry**, and lacrimal system evaluation. Grade Gain covers the ones this question omits — `opqb-t20-800` (corneal epithelial defects) and `opqb-t20-802` (Seidel test for an aqueous leak). This stem asks only whether the two offered uses are genuine, and both are.\n\n⚠️ **Repairs recorded.** The stem prints **\"flourescien dye\"** and option C prints **\"A& B\"** — with the space after the ampersand only, where every other occurrence in the chapter (including `opmcq-c20-12`'s option C) prints **\"A & B\"**. Both are repaired here; the dye's chapter-wide spelling inconsistency is recorded in `opmcq-c20-3`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Cornea.)*",
    objective:"Recognise that topical fluorescein is used both to measure tear film stability (TBUT) and to test lacrimal drainage patency, and place those two uses within the book's four topical indications.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.129 (Ch.20 Q8). The stem prints "flourescien dye" and option C prints "A& B"; both repaired here and recorded in the explanation.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:"Statins can cause which of the following adverse effects?",
    options:["Cataract","Glaucoma","Paralytic strabismus","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Cataract — as keyed.** ⚠️ **This is the chapter's sharpest divergence between the key and the page it is drawn from, and it must be read carefully rather than memorised.**\n\n**What `ophthalmology.pdf` p.270 actually prints,** under the heading *HMG-COA reductase inhibitors (Statins)*, in three consecutive bullets:\n\n1. Statins are **cholesterol-lowering agents**.\n2. **Long-term use of these drugs in humans has NOT been shown to increase the risk of cataract.**\n3. **Concurrent use of simvastatin with erythromycin has been associated with an increased risk of cataract formation.**\n\nThe polarity **reverses between bullets 2 and 3**. Statins *alone*: no demonstrated cataract risk. Simvastatin *plus erythromycin*: increased cataract risk. Both sentences are on the same page, adjacent, in the same section.\n\n**Why the key is A.** The stem asks, without qualification, what statins *can* cause. Cataract is the **only ocular adverse effect the book associates with this drug class at all** — the section names nothing else. Reading the stem as \"which of these has any printed association\", A is the sole candidate; reading it strictly as \"statins alone\", the printed answer would be E. The bank keys **A**, and per project rule the printed key stands: **a defective or arguable key is recorded, never disputed.** What is recorded is that the association the book supports is the **combination** one, not statin monotherapy.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Cataract** | ✅ Keyed. Supported by p.270 **only through the simvastatin-plus-erythromycin bullet**; the preceding bullet explicitly denies the risk for long-term statin use on its own. |\n| Glaucoma | ✗ Wrong drug class. **Corticosteroids** are the drugs on p.270 associated with **elevated IOP (steroid-induced glaucoma)** in susceptible individuals, and p.261 lists glaucoma among the complications of topical ocular steroids. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-793` keys retinopathy/glaucoma/cataract as *all of the above* — for steroids, not statins. |\n| Paralytic strabismus | ✗ Not attributed to statins, or to any drug, anywhere in chapter 20. No systemic medication in the p.269–270 list is given an ocular motility effect. |\n| All of the above | ✗ Would require glaucoma and paralytic strabismus to be statin effects; neither is. |\n| None of the above | ✗ ⚠️ Defensible on bullet 2 alone, and a student who read only that bullet will choose it. The bank's key is A. |\n\n**Corroboration attempted and reported, not assumed.** The cataract lectures do **not** name statins among cataractogenic drugs: `L35,36) Chronic visual loss.txt` gives the cataractogenic drug list as **chlorpromazine, miotics, myleran, amiodarone, gold**, and `L18) Cataract II.txt` lists medication causes as **steroids, alpha-blockers (IFIS), amiodarone**. Neither deck mentions statins in either direction, so the lectures neither support nor contradict the key — the whole question rests on p.270.\n\n⚠️ **The examinable takeaway, stated in the safe direction:** statins alone are *not* shown to cause cataract; the risk appears when **simvastatin is combined with erythromycin**. Get that pair the wrong way round and the statement becomes clinically wrong.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Systemic Diseases.)*",
    objective:"Associate statins with cataract as the bank keys it, while distinguishing the book's two opposing statements — no cataract risk from long-term statin use alone, increased risk from simvastatin combined with erythromycin — and assign glaucoma to corticosteroids instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.129 (Ch.20 Q9, the last question on the page). Key printed as A; the divergence from ophthalmology.pdf p.270 is recorded in the explanation and the key is left as printed.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-insid',
    stem:"Which of the following is an adverse effect of systemic anti-VEGF therapy?",
    options:["Myocardial infarction","Hypertension","GI perforation","Kidney disease","All of the above"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**All of the above.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.263 lists the adverse events reported with the **systemic** administration of anti-VEGF agents as: **thromboembolic events, myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, gastrointestinal perforations, and kidney disease**. Every one of the four options is on that list, so the collective option is the key.\n\n**Why the list looks like that.** VEGF is not only the driver of pathological ocular neovascularisation; it maintains normal vascular endothelium throughout the body. Suppress it systemically and the damage follows the vasculature — arterial (MI, stroke), pressure regulation (hypertension), the gut wall (perforation) and the glomerulus (kidney disease).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Myocardial infarction | ✗ True but incomplete — named on p.263. |\n| Hypertension | ✗ True but incomplete — named on p.263. |\n| GI perforation | ✗ True but incomplete — named on p.263. |\n| Kidney disease | ✗ True but incomplete — named on p.263. |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ All four appear in the book's own list. |\n\n⚠️ **The intravitreal route does not make this irrelevant.** p.263 closes the section with the point the question depends on: **all the intravitreal anti-VEGF agents have been reported to reach detectable levels in the systemic circulation** and may significantly suppress systemic VEGF, *so they may be implicated in the occurrence of potential systemic adverse events*. An injection into the eye is not a sealed compartment. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-820` asks the same question of a patient being treated for wet AMD.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"List the systemic adverse events of anti-VEGF therapy — thromboembolism, myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, GI perforation and kidney disease — and explain why intravitreal injection does not exclude them.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.130 (Ch.20 Q10, the first question on the page).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common use of hyperosmotic agents?",
    options:["Primary open angle glaucoma","Secondary open angle glaucoma","Acute angle closure glaucoma","Chronic angle closure glaucoma","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Acute angle closure glaucoma.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.267 states it directly: systemic hyperosmotic agents **draw water out of the body tissues into the plasma** and consequently **quickly reduce IOP** by drawing aqueous out of the eye — and *their most common use in ophthalmology is in cases of acute angle closure glaucoma*. The summary table on p.268 repeats it in the book's own shorthand: *their **MC** use … is in cases of acute angle closure glaucoma*.\n\n**The agents,** listed on p.264 as class VII of the glaucoma medications, with the only concentrations the chapter prints for any anti-glaucoma drug:\n\n| Agent | Route | Concentration |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Glycerin | Oral | **50%** |\n| Isosorbide | Oral | **45%** |\n| Mannitol | **Intravenous** | **20%** |\n\n**Why this indication and not the others.** Hyperosmotics act fast and briefly, by osmosis rather than on aqueous dynamics — they neither reduce production nor open an outflow route. That makes them a rescue measure for an eye whose pressure must fall *now*, and useless as maintenance. Acute angle closure is the emergency that fits: `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry.txt` puts **IV mannitol** and **oral hypertonic glycerol** in its medical treatment list for acute angle closure glaucoma, alongside systemic acetazolamide, topical beta-blockers, alpha agonists, CAIs, miotics and steroids — and follows it with **laser iridotomy** as the definitive step.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Primary open angle glaucoma | ✗ A chronic disease managed with daily topical drops for life (p.263: *glaucoma is a lifelong disease; drops must be taken every day*). A short-acting osmotic load has no place in it. |\n| Secondary open angle glaucoma | ✗ Same objection; treatment is directed at the underlying cause and at long-term pressure control. |\n| **Acute angle closure glaucoma** | ✅ The book's stated most common use — a rapid, temporary pressure drop in an ocular emergency. |\n| Chronic angle closure glaucoma | ✗ Chronic by definition, and therefore not the emergency these agents exist for. |\n| None of the above | ✗ C is printed. |\n\n⚠️ **Two questions in the shipped corpus share this option menu and are not duplicates of it.** `opmcq-c11-7` gives a clinical vignette and asks for the **diagnosis**; Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-812` asks what **decongestants** may precipitate (also acute angle-closure glaucoma, `opmcq-c20-12`). A shared menu pairs questions by topic; it does not make them the same question. Side effects of this class: `opmcq-c20-14`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Name acute angle closure glaucoma as the commonest indication for hyperosmotic agents, list the three agents with their concentrations, and explain why an osmotic agent cannot serve chronic glaucoma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.130 (Ch.20 Q11).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Which of the following is among the complications of ocular decongestants?",
    options:["Rebound vasodilatation","Acute angle closure glaucoma","A & B","Neither A nor B"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A & B — both.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.261 heads the block *the most frequent complication of ocular decongestants arises from overuse* and then prints exactly two numbered items:\n\n| # | Complication (p.261) | Mechanism |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Rebound vasodilation** of conjunctival vessels — *when used in excess, these preparations can increase rather than decrease the redness of the eyes* | Loss of vasoconstrictor effect on withdrawal, in vessels chronically clamped |\n| 2 | **Acute angle-closure glaucoma** *in rare instances*, precipitated **in susceptible eyes** by sympathomimetic drugs | These agents are adrenergic, so **they can dilate the pupil**, and a mid-dilated pupil closes a narrow angle |\n\nBoth are printed as complications of the same class, so the collective option is the key.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Rebound vasodilatation | ✗ True but incomplete — item 1, and the **most frequent** one (that is the point of `opmcq-c20-25`, which asks for the commonest rather than the list). |\n| Acute angle closure glaucoma | ✗ True but incomplete — item 2, rare and restricted to susceptible eyes. |\n| **A & B** | ✅ Both are printed complications of ocular decongestants. |\n| Neither A nor B | ✗ Both are. |\n\n⚠️ **The qualifier the book attaches to item 2 is examinable in its own right:** these drugs *may be used without harm by patients with **chronic open-angle glaucoma**, because they do not produce a rise in IOP if the filtration angle is open* (p.261). The danger is anatomical — a **narrow** angle plus a dilating pupil — not pharmacological pressure elevation. The same mechanism drives `opmcq-c20-29`, where an anticholinergic does the dilating.\n\nGrade Gain covers the two halves separately: `opqb-t20-812` keys decongestants precipitating **acute angle-closure glaucoma**, and `opqb-t20-817` keys **tetrahydrozoline** as the cause of rebound redness.\n\n⚠️ **Printed spacing note:** option C prints **\"A & B\"** here with normal spacing, in contrast to `opmcq-c20-8`, whose option C prints **\"A& B\"**. Both are transcribed and the defective one repaired there.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Glaucoma.)*",
    objective:"Name both printed complications of ocular decongestants — rebound vasodilation and, rarely, acute angle-closure glaucoma in susceptible eyes — and explain why open-angle glaucoma is not endangered by them.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.130 (Ch.20 Q12).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:"Which of the following is an adverse effect of local cycloplegics?",
    options:["Yellowish discoloration of skin","Corneal ulceration","Delirium","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Delirium.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.259 prints a boxed list headed *Systemic toxicity of cycloplegics* (**especially in children**), with five entries:\n\n| # | Systemic toxicity of cycloplegics (p.259) |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Increased body temperature |\n| 2 | Flushing (reddening) of skin |\n| 3 | Dryness of skin and mouth |\n| 4 | Irregular rapid pulse |\n| 5 | **Hallucinations, confusion, and delirium** |\n\nThis is the classic **antimuscarinic** toxidrome, and it follows from the mechanism: cycloplegics are **cholinergic-blocking (parasympatholytic)** drugs that dilate the pupil by **paralysing the sphincter pupillae** and paralyse accommodation by paralysing the ciliary muscle (p.259). Enough of the drug is absorbed from the conjunctival sac to block muscarinic receptors elsewhere, the central ones included — hence the delirium. \"Local\" describes where the drop goes, not where the drug stays.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Yellowish discoloration of skin | ✗ Belongs to **intravenous fluorescein**, where discolouration of skin and urine fades within 36 hours (p.258, `opmcq-c20-6`). Nothing to do with cycloplegics. |\n| Corneal ulceration | ✗ Belongs to **topical local anaesthetics**: prolonged or repeated use is toxic to the corneal epithelium, inhibits mitosis and cellular migration, and **can lead to corneal ulceration and scarring** (p.258, `opmcq-c20-19`). Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-805` keys exactly that for a week of self-administered tetracaine. |\n| **Delirium** | ✅ Item 5 of the systemic toxicity list, and the danger that makes cycloplegic dosing in children a deliberate decision. |\n| None of the above | ✗ C is printed. |\n\n⚠️ **A structural point on p.259 that is easy to misread.** The separate *Systemic side effects* row of the five-drug table — nausea, vomiting, pallor, vasomotor collapse, *rare because of its brief duration of action* — sits in the **tropicamide column only**; the cyclopentolate, homatropine, scopolamine and atropine cells of that row are blank. The five-item list above is the one that applies to **cycloplegics as a class**, and it is the one this question tests.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Recognise delirium as part of the antimuscarinic systemic toxicity of cycloplegics, especially in children, and assign yellow skin discolouration to IV fluorescein and corneal ulceration to topical anaesthetic abuse.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.130 (Ch.20 Q13).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Side effects of hyperosmotic agents include all of the following except?",
    options:["Thirst","Hypovolemia","Headache","Disorientation"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hypovolemia.** The book prints *hypervolemia* — the exact opposite. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.267 lists six side effects of hyperosmotic agents:\n\n| # | Side effect (p.267) | Note as printed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Thirst** | *Drinking counteracts the effect of these drugs* |\n| 2 | Headache | — |\n| 3 | Disorientation | — |\n| 4 | **Hypervolemia** | Can result in cardiac problems (**congestive heart failure**) |\n| 5 | Nausea or vomiting | Oral hyperosmotics are extremely sweet |\n| 6 | **Hyperglycaemia in diabetics** | Specifically **glycerine** |\n\nThe distractor is built on a single syllable, and the mechanism decides it. These agents work by **drawing water out of the body tissues into the plasma** (p.267) — that is what lets them pull aqueous out of the eye. Expanding the plasma volume is the *mechanism*, so the circulatory complication must be **hyper**volaemia, with the failing heart unable to handle the load. Hypovolaemia would describe the reverse and would make the drug's own action impossible.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Thirst | ✗ Printed side effect 1 — and clinically important, because drinking to relieve it undoes the treatment. |\n| **Hypovolemia** | ✅ The exception. The book prints **hypervolemia**, leading to congestive heart failure. |\n| Headache | ✗ Printed side effect 2. |\n| Disorientation | ✗ Printed side effect 3. |\n\n⚠️ **Two side effects this four-option menu leaves out are the ones most likely to be asked elsewhere:** the extreme sweetness of the oral agents causing nausea and vomiting, and **glycerine causing hyperglycaemia in diabetics** — the reason the choice between oral glycerin and IV mannitol is not arbitrary in a diabetic with acute angle closure. The agents, their concentrations and their indication are in `opmcq-c20-11`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"List the side effects of hyperosmotic agents and identify hypervolaemia — not hypovolaemia — as the circulatory complication, reasoning from the drugs' mechanism of drawing water into the plasma.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.130 (Ch.20 Q14, the last question on the page).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:"Viagra can cause which of the following ocular side effects?",
    options:["Blurred vision","Transient impairment of color discrimination","Loss of accommodation","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Transient impairment of colour discrimination.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.270 devotes its last section to **sildenafil (Viagra)** and its relatives — **tadalafil (Cialis), vardenafil (Levitra, Staxyn) and avanafil (Stendra)** — all **cGMP-specific phosphodiesterase 5 inhibitors** used primarily for erectile dysfunction. It prints three statements about the eye:\n\n| Statement (p.270) | |\n| --- | --- |\n| At the time of **peak plasma levels**, patients may experience **transient, mild impairment of colour discrimination**, often noted as a **blue colour tinge of vision** | The keyed effect — note *transient*, *mild*, and tied to peak levels |\n| **No long-term ocular side effects have been identified to date** | The class is not known to leave permanent damage |\n| There have been a **few case reports** of **nonarteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy** and **central serous retinopathy** | Rare, and reported as cases rather than as established effects |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Blurred vision | ✗ Not printed for this class. The book's visual disturbance here is chromatic, not a loss of acuity — the patient sees the world tinged blue, not out of focus. |\n| **Transient impairment of color discrimination** | ✅ The book's phrase almost word for word. |\n| Loss of accommodation | ✗ Belongs to the **cycloplegics** (p.259): cholinergic-blocking drugs paralyse the ciliary muscle, so near vision blurs until the drug wears off. PDE-5 inhibitors have no antimuscarinic action. |\n| All of the above | ✗ Would require A and C to be true; neither is printed for this class. |\n| None of the above | ✗ B is printed. |\n\nGrade Gain's `opqb-t20-830` keys the same effect in the book's other wording — *sildenafil causes* **blue vision** — against permanent blindness, red vision and central scotoma.\n\n⚠️ **Why PDE-5 inhibition tints vision blue is not stated by this book**: PDE-5 inhibitors have weak cross-inhibition of **PDE-6**, the phototransduction enzyme of the retinal photoreceptors, and the effect is dose- and peak-level-related — *this mechanism is **not taken from the course material***, which gives the observation without explaining it. What the book does supply is the timing: the effect appears at **peak plasma levels** and is transient, which is why reassurance rather than withdrawal is the usual response.\n\n⚠️ **Repair recorded:** option B prints **\"discrimmination\"**; repaired here to *discrimination*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Identify transient impairment of colour discrimination (a blue tinge at peak plasma levels) as the ocular effect of PDE-5 inhibitors, and separate it from loss of accommodation, which belongs to cycloplegics.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.131 (Ch.20 Q15, the first question on the page). Option B prints "discrimmination"; repaired here.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"All of the following medications are among alpha 2 adrenergic agonists except?",
    options:["Apraclonidine","Epinephrine","Dipivefrin","Pilocarpine","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Pilocarpine.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.264 sets out the book's classification of glaucoma medications, and the **alpha-2 adrenergic agonist** class contains exactly four names:\n\n| Alpha-2 adrenergic agonists (p.264) | Trade names as printed |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Brimonidine tartrate** | Alphagan-P, Alphanova, Brimonidine |\n| **Apraclonidine** | — |\n| **Epinephrine** | — |\n| **Dipivefrin** | — |\n\nPilocarpine is not among them. It is the sole member of the **cholinergic stimulating drugs** class (p.264, printed as *Isoptocarpine, Ocucarpine*), and it works by a completely different mechanism: it **lowers IOP by increasing aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork** (p.265), where the alpha-2 agonists work by **decreased aqueous production plus increased uveoscleral outflow** (p.266). Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-823` keys pilocarpine's route as the trabecular meshwork, and `opqb-t20-824` keys the alpha-2 class from its side-effect profile.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Apraclonidine | ✗ Printed in the class on p.264. |\n| Epinephrine | ✗ Printed in the class on p.264. |\n| Dipivefrin | ✗ Printed in the class on p.264. |\n| **Pilocarpine** | ✅ The exception — a cholinergic stimulating drug, a different class with a different mechanism. |\n| None of the above | ✗ An exception exists. |\n\n⚠️ **The book's own class list is worth reading as printed, and recorded as printed.** Epinephrine is a non-selective adrenergic agonist and dipivefrin is its prodrug; neither is alpha-2 *selective* in the way brimonidine is (p.266 calls brimonidine *a relatively selective alpha-2 agonist*). The book nevertheless files both under the alpha-2 heading on p.264, and the bank follows the book. Recorded, not corrected — the answer to this question does not turn on it, because pilocarpine is not adrenergic at all.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **Never quote a bare class number from this chapter.** The alpha-2 agonists are class **III** on p.264 (roman I–VII), class **3** in the per-class discussion on pp.265–267 (arabic 1–6, a *different* order, with Combinations dropped), and the **first column** of the summary table on p.268 (a third order again). \"Class 3\" means nothing here unless the page is named.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Assign apraclonidine, epinephrine and dipivefrin to the book's alpha-2 adrenergic agonist class and exclude pilocarpine as a cholinergic stimulating drug acting through the trabecular meshwork.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.131 (Ch.20 Q16).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Brimonidine tartrate is known to cause all of the following side effects except?",
    options:["Local allergic reaction","Hyperactivity","Oral dryness","Fatigue","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Hyperactivity.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.266 says brimonidine's systemic side effects **are few**, and lists them:\n\n| # | Systemic side effect of brimonidine tartrate (p.266) |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Oral dryness** |\n| 2 | **Headache** |\n| 3 | **Fatigue** |\n| 4 | **Drowsiness** — because the drug is **lipid soluble and crosses the blood-brain barrier** |\n| 5 | **In infants**, the risk of **severe hypotension and apnoea** |\n| 6 | **Local allergic reaction** |\n\nThe list is repeated unchanged in the summary table on p.268. **Drowsiness, not hyperactivity**, is the central effect — and the mechanism is printed alongside it: the drug is lipid soluble and crosses the blood–brain barrier, where central alpha-2 stimulation is **sedating**. Hyperactivity is the exact reverse of the drug's known CNS action, which is why it is the exception.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Local allergic reaction | ✗ Item 6. The commonest reason brimonidine is abandoned in practice. |\n| **Hyperactivity** | ✅ The exception. The book's CNS effect is **drowsiness**. |\n| Oral dryness | ✗ Item 1. |\n| Fatigue | ✗ Item 3. |\n| None of the above | ✗ An exception exists. |\n\n⚠️ **The infant warning is the one that kills.** p.266 records **severe hypotension and apnoea** in infants — the reason brimonidine is avoided in small children even though it is an ordinary topical drop in adults. It is not offered here, but it belongs to the same list.\n\nGrade Gain's `opqb-t20-824` runs the identification the other way: a 34-year-old glaucoma patient with **dry mouth, fatigue, headache and noticeable drowsiness** on a drug that crosses the blood–brain barrier and lowers IOP by both reducing production and increasing outflow — keyed **alpha-2 agonist**. The same four symptoms, used to name the class instead of to spot the impostor. Brimonidine's mechanism is set out in `opmcq-c20-16`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"List brimonidine's systemic side effects — oral dryness, headache, fatigue, drowsiness, local allergy, and hypotension with apnoea in infants — and reject hyperactivity as the opposite of its central sedating action.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.131 (Ch.20 Q17).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following conditions is associated with the use of amiodarone?",
    options:["Whorl-shaped pigmented corneal deposits","Arteritic AION","Bull's eye macular lesions","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Whorl-shaped pigmented corneal deposits.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.269 opens the chapter's second half — the banner section *Examples of Ocular Side Effects of Systemic Medications* — with amiodarone, **a cardiac arrhythmia drug**, and gives it three associations:\n\n| Association (p.269) | Detail as printed |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Corneal deposits** | **Whorl-shaped, pigmented deposits in the corneal epithelium** — *cornea verticillate* (sic; the usual form is *cornea verticillata*) or **vortex keratopathy**. **Dosage-related and reversible** if the dose is reduced or the drug stopped. They **rarely cause visual symptoms**. |\n| **Optic neuropathy** | **Non-arteritic AION** — mildly decreased vision, visual field defects, **bilateral optic disc swelling** |\n| **Intracranial hypertension** | Listed without further detail |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Whorl-shaped pigmented corneal deposits** | ✅ The book's first and most characteristic association, and the sign that has a name of its own. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-828` keys amiodarone for **vortex keratopathy** against hydroxychloroquine, atropine and mannitol. |\n| Arteritic AION | ✗ ⚠️ **The trap, and it turns on one word.** Amiodarone's optic neuropathy is **NON-arteritic** AION. *Arteritic* AION is the ischaemic optic neuropathy of **giant cell arteritis** — an inflammatory arteritis, not a drug effect. Option B is a real entity attached to the wrong cause. |\n| Bull's eye macular lesions | ✗ Belongs to **chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine** on the same page (p.269): the retinopathy is *insidious, slowly progressive and usually irreversible*, and the typical bull's-eye lesions **do not become visible ophthalmoscopically until serious retinal damage has already occurred**. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-829` keys hydroxychloroquine for bull's-eye retinopathy. |\n| All of the above | ✗ Fails on B alone. Because B is wrong, the collective option cannot be right — this is precisely why the writer inserted *arteritic*. |\n| None of the above | ✗ A is printed. |\n\n⚠️ **The contrast between the two corneal-deposit drugs on p.269 is worth holding.** Amiodarone's deposits are **dosage-related, reversible and rarely symptomatic**; chloroquine's corneal deposits are **usually asymptomatic but can produce glare and photophobia** and also **regress when the drug is discontinued**. The corneas of both recover. It is chloroquine's **retina** that does not — *much more serious*, in the book's words.\n\n`L35,36) Chronic visual loss.txt` names amiodarone in its list of cataractogenic drugs (with chlorpromazine, miotics, myleran and gold), and `L18) Cataract II.txt` lists it among medication causes of cataract — a fourth association the book's amiodarone section does not carry, and one not offered here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: Optic Nerve.)*",
    objective:"Attribute whorl-shaped pigmented corneal epithelial deposits (vortex keratopathy) to amiodarone, recognise its optic neuropathy as non-arteritic AION, and assign bull's-eye macular lesions to chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.131 (Ch.20 Q18).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cornea',
    stem:"Which of the following is among the side effects that accompany the use of local anaesthetics?",
    options:["Corneal epithelial defect","Keratoconus","Corneal opacification","Retinal detachment"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Corneal epithelial defect.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.258 lists three adverse reactions to topical local anaesthetics, and the second is the answer:\n\n| # | Adverse reaction (p.258) |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | Early stinging sensation |\n| 2 | **Corneal epithelial damage** — with *prolonged or uncontrolled use* |\n| 3 | Local or systemic allergic reactions (rare) |\n\nThe same page explains the mechanism in its closing N.B.: these agents **should never be prescribed for prolonged or repeated use by patients because they are toxic to the corneal epithelium — they inhibit mitosis and cellular migration**. Both of those are exactly what an epithelium needs in order to close a defect, so the drug that numbs the surface also prevents it from healing. The book adds the practical corollary: patients must be told **not to rub their eyes for at least 10 minutes** after a topical anaesthetic, to avoid inadvertent corneal abrasions while the surface is insensate.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Corneal epithelial defect** | ✅ The book's own adverse reaction, and the direct consequence of inhibited mitosis and migration in an anaesthetised epithelium. |\n| Keratoconus | ✗ A progressive ectatic corneal dystrophy — a structural thinning disease, not a drug effect. No drug in chapter 20 is associated with it. |\n| Corneal opacification | ✗ ⚠️ The near miss. p.258 does say that prolonged or repeated use **can lead to corneal ulceration and scarring**, and scarring is opacification — but that is the *late outcome of an untreated abuse*, downstream of the epithelial breakdown. The adverse reaction the book actually lists for the drug class is **epithelial damage**, and the question asks for the side effect that accompanies use. |\n| Retinal detachment | ✗ Posterior segment; a topical drop does not reach it, and no mechanism connects the two. |\n\nGrade Gain's `opqb-t20-805` takes the same fact to its endpoint: a patient self-using **tetracaine for a week** for pain relief, with **corneal ulceration** keyed as the most serious risk. This question catches the process one step earlier.\n\n⚠️ **Repair recorded:** the stem prints **\"is the among the side effects\"** — a stray *the* — repaired here. ⚠️ **Not repaired, and deliberately so:** the stem also prints **\"anaesthetics\"**, the British spelling, in a chapter that otherwise prints American forms (*anesthesia* in `opmcq-c20-1`, *color* in `opmcq-c20-15`, *diarrhea* in `opmcq-c20-21`, *edema*). It is what the page prints and it is left exactly as printed.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Name corneal epithelial damage as the adverse reaction to prolonged topical anaesthetic use, and explain it through inhibition of epithelial mitosis and cell migration.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.131 (Ch.20 Q19, the last question on the page; its printed "Answer: A" is the first line of book p.132). The stem prints "is the among the side effects"; the stray article is repaired here and the British "anaesthetics" is left exactly as printed.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:"Which of the following is the abnormal (cutoff) value of fluorescein dye disappearance test?",
    options:[">5 seconds",">5 minutes","<5 seconds",">10 seconds"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**More than 5 minutes.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.257 gives the dye disappearance test in two lines: **normally little or no dye remains in the conjunctival sac 5 minutes after instillation**, and **dye retention of more than 5 minutes indicates inadequate lacrimal drainage**. (The page prints *Normaly* and *indicated*; both are the book's own spellings and are recorded here, not corrected in the sense of being attributed elsewhere.)\n\nThe test is the crude first step of the patency sequence, before the Jones tests: instil the dye, wait, and look at how much is still sitting in the sac. Retained dye means the tears are not draining. The complete lacrimal-patency table — dye disappearance, Jones I and Jones II — is set out once in `opmcq-c20-3`.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| >5 seconds | ✗ Right number, wrong unit. The trap depends on the reader carrying \"5\" and dropping \"minutes\". |\n| **>5 minutes** | ✅ The printed cutoff for inadequate lacrimal drainage. |\n| <5 seconds | ✗ Wrong unit and wrong direction — a *faster* disappearance is the normal finding, not the abnormal one. |\n| >10 seconds | ✗ ⚠️ Borrowed from the wrong test. **10 seconds** is the TBUT threshold on the same page: *a TBUT measuring less than 10 seconds is indicative of dry eye*. Both numbers sit in the same table, so the two get swapped in both directions. |\n\n⚠️ **Hold the two numbers apart by what each one measures.** TBUT times how long the tear film survives on the surface — a **short** time is abnormal, and it is measured in **seconds**. The dye disappearance test times how long the dye takes to leave the sac — a **long** time is abnormal, and it is measured in **minutes**. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-803` tests the TBUT definition itself (last blink → first dark spot).\n\n⚠️ **Spelling note:** this stem spells the dye **\"fluorescein\"** correctly, unlike the option menus of `opmcq-c20-3`, `opmcq-c20-4`, `opmcq-c20-6` and `opmcq-c20-8`, which print *Flourescien*. Both forms are printed in this one chapter; the inconsistency is recorded in `opmcq-c20-3`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State the abnormal dye disappearance test cutoff as retention of dye beyond 5 minutes, and keep it distinct from the 10-second TBUT threshold printed on the same page.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.132 (Ch.20 Q20, the first full question on the page — the page opens with Q19\'s answer line).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is an adverse effect of beta-blockers?",
    options:["Bronchospasm and bronchial asthma","Diarrhea","Urticaria","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All of the above.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.265 divides the systemic side effects of topical beta-adrenergic blockers into three groups, and the three options are one item from each:\n\n| Group (p.265) | Contents |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Pulmonary** | **Bronchospasm, bronchial asthma, dyspnoea** — these drugs are therefore **contraindicated in asthma or COPD**, and *several deaths have been reported secondary to the pulmonary complications of topically administered timolol* |\n| **Cardiac** | Bradycardia, conduction arrhythmias, **hypotension** and fluid retention; they may **precipitate or worsen heart failure** (`opmcq-c20-24`) |\n| **Other systemic** | Headache, insomnia, amnesia, depression and confusion · **Diarrhoea and nausea** · **Urticaria** |\n\nBecause options A, B and C are each printed on that page, the collective option is the key.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Bronchospasm and bronchial asthma | ✗ True but incomplete — the pulmonary group, and the group that has killed. |\n| Diarrhea | ✗ True but incomplete — printed under *other systemic side effects*. This is the option the question is really testing: a gastrointestinal effect from an eye drop looks implausible until the absorption route is remembered. |\n| Urticaria | ✗ True but incomplete — also printed under *other systemic side effects*. |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ All three are on p.265, and the same three groups are reproduced in the summary table on p.268. |\n| None of the above | ✗ All three are printed. |\n\n⚠️ **How an eye drop reaches the lungs, the heart and the gut** is stated on p.263, and it is the premise of this whole half of the chapter: any drug instilled in the conjunctival cul-de-sac may be absorbed systemically by the **conjunctiva, the nasopharyngeal mucosa, or the gastrointestinal tract (after saliva is swallowed)** — reaching the circulation without a first pass through the liver. The same page gives the countermeasure: after instilling glaucoma drops, **close the eyes and press gently on the nasal corner of the palpebral fissure for 30–60 seconds** to occlude the lacrimal passages. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-821` keys exactly that manoeuvre for an 80-year-old on timolol with dyspnoea and bradycardia.\n\nThe cardio-selective alternative is `opmcq-c20-22`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Group the systemic side effects of topical beta-blockers into pulmonary, cardiac and other systemic effects, and explain the conjunctival and nasopharyngeal absorption that produces them.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.132 (Ch.20 Q21).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is a beta-blocker that doesn't contribute to the development of pulmonary and cardiac adverse effects?",
    options:["Propranolol","Timolol","Betaxolol","All of the above","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Betaxolol.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.265 introduces it as the answer to the problem set out in `opmcq-c20-21`: *a **cardio-selective B1-adrenergic antagonist**, **betaxolol**, was developed to avoid the pulmonary complications of timolol.* It **may be as effective as nonselective beta-adrenergic antagonists in lowering IOP**. The summary table on p.268 files it under its own grey-barred heading, *Cardio-selective BB: **Betaxolol***, opposite the non-selective group.\n\n**The pharmacology in one line:** bronchial smooth muscle carries **beta-2** receptors; the heart is predominantly **beta-1**. A drug selective for beta-1 spares the airway, which is why cardio-selectivity is what makes a beta-blocker usable in a patient with asthma or COPD.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Propranolol | ✗ A **non-selective** beta-blocker, and in any case not one of the five ocular beta-blockers the book lists on p.264 (timolol, levobunolol, betaxolol, metipranolol, carteolol). It is the archetypal drug to avoid in asthma. |\n| Timolol | ✗ The **non-selective** agent whose complications betaxolol was developed to avoid. p.265 records **several deaths** from the pulmonary complications of topical timolol. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-822` puts the same swap into a vignette: a 65-year-old on timolol with worsening cough and wheeze needs a safer drug. |\n| **Betaxolol** | ✅ The cardio-selective beta-1 antagonist, named on p.264 (Betoptic, Apixol, Epitaxol), on p.265 and again on p.268. |\n| All of the above | ✗ Two of the three are non-selective. |\n| None of the above | ✗ Betaxolol is printed. |\n\n⚠️ **The stem is stronger than the book, and the difference matters clinically.** \"Doesn't contribute to the development of pulmonary and cardiac adverse effects\" reads as an absolute; p.265 is careful not to say that — *however, pulmonary effects have **occasionally been noted**, and caution should be used when this drug is employed in patients with excessive impairment of pulmonary function.* Cardio-selectivity is relative, not absolute, and a severe asthmatic can still react to betaxolol. Recorded, key undisturbed: betaxolol is unarguably the best of the five, and it is the only cardio-selective agent on the page.\n\n⚠️ **Where the name is printed.** The project brief for this chapter warned that betaxolol is named only in the p.268 summary table and not in the body text. That is **not what the cache shows**: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.265 names betaxolol explicitly, in dark red, in the body text. Both pages carry it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Identify betaxolol as the cardio-selective beta-1 antagonist developed to avoid timolol's pulmonary complications, and qualify its selectivity as relative rather than absolute.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.132 (Ch.20 Q22).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding administration of (ethambutol)?",
    options:["It can cause optic neuropathy","It mostly causes irreversible vision impairment even if it's stopped","It occasionally causes vision loss","A & B","A & C"],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**A & C.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.270 gives ethambutol four lines, and the key is assembled from them:\n\n| Statement (p.270) | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Ethambutol is useful in the **chemotherapy of tuberculosis** | The context — a drug the patient will be taking for months |\n| As a side effect, ethambutol **can result in optic neuropathy** | Option **A**, verbatim |\n| The onset of vision loss may be **within 1 month** of starting the drug | Early, and therefore worth warning about at prescription |\n| **Recovery usually occurs when the drug is stopped**, but it may take **months**; **occasionally, vision loss is permanent** | Refutes option **B**; supplies option **C** |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| It can cause optic neuropathy | ✗ True, but half the key. |\n| It mostly causes irreversible vision impairment even if it's stopped | ✗ **False, and the discriminator.** The book says the opposite twice over: recovery **usually** occurs on stopping the drug, and permanence is **occasional**. \"Mostly irreversible\" inverts both adverbs. |\n| It occasionally causes vision loss | ✗ True, but half the key. Read against the page, *occasionally* attaches to the **permanent** vision loss that survives stopping the drug — the residue left after the usual recovery. |\n| A & B | ✗ Contains the false statement. |\n| **A & C** | ✅ The two true statements. |\n\n**The clinical shape of it.** Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-827` prints the presentation this question omits: a 55-year-old on long-term treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis with **progressive, painless, bilateral loss of central vision over several weeks**, a fundus that may look **normal initially**, and field testing showing central defects — keyed **optic neuropathy**. A normal-looking fundus with falling central acuity is the trap; the disease is retrobulbar at first.\n\n⚠️ **Why the reversibility clause is the examinable part.** Ethambutol optic neuropathy is one of the few drug-induced optic neuropathies that **recovers**, and it recovers *because the drug is stopped*. That makes early detection worth something — which is the practical reason the question exists. *(Colour vision and visual field monitoring during ethambutol therapy is standard practice but is **not taken from the course material**: p.270 prints no dose, no duration threshold and no monitoring protocol for ethambutol, in contrast to the baseline screening it does specify for hydroxychloroquine on p.269.)*\n\n⚠️ **Repairs recorded:** option B prints **\"irrerversible\"** and **\"even if its stopped\"** without the apostrophe; both are repaired here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State that ethambutol can cause optic neuropathy which usually recovers when the drug is stopped, with vision loss only occasionally permanent, and reject the claim that the impairment is mostly irreversible.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.132 (Ch.20 Q23). Option B prints "irrerversible" and "even if its stopped"; both repaired here.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following is a medication used for treating glaucoma that can cause hypotension?",
    options:["Pilocarpine","Timolol","Brimonidine","Latanoprost"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Timolol.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.265 lists the cardiac side effects of topical beta-adrenergic antagonists as **bradycardia, conduction arrhythmias, hypotension and fluid retention**, and adds that they may **precipitate or worsen heart failure** and must be used with caution *if bradycardia or systemic hypotension would adversely affect the patient*. Timolol is the book's headline non-selective beta-blocker — *highly effective and widely used* — and therefore the class's representative here.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Pilocarpine | ✗ Its systemic effects are **cholinergic**: lacrimation, salivation, perspiration, nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea (p.265). The book adds that they are rare, occurring only at **5 to 10 times the usual ocular dosage**. Hypotension is not among them. |\n| **Timolol** | ✅ Hypotension is printed among the cardiac side effects of the beta-blockers on p.265 and again in the summary table on p.268. |\n| Brimonidine | ✗ ⚠️ **A real competitor, and the reason this entry is worth reading twice.** p.266 states that **in infants** brimonidine carries the risk of producing **severe hypotension and apnoea**. Its adult systemic list (oral dryness, headache, fatigue, drowsiness, local allergy — `opmcq-c20-17`) contains no hypotension. So brimonidine causes hypotension *in one specific population*, whereas the beta-blockers cause it as a general cardiac effect. The bank keys **B**, and per project rule the printed key stands; the overlap is recorded here rather than disputed. |\n| Latanoprost | ✗ A prostaglandin analogue, the class of which p.266 says **no major systemic toxic effects have been reported** — its trouble is entirely ocular and periocular (`opmcq-c20-7`). |\n\n**How the question would read unambiguously:** *in an adult*, timolol is the only one of the four with hypotension as a listed systemic effect. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-821` and `opqb-t20-822` both build vignettes on timolol's cardiopulmonary toxicity — bradycardia and dyspnoea in an 80-year-old, wheeze in a 65-year-old — confirming which drug the module treats as the cardiovascular offender.\n\n⚠️ Note that the mechanism runs through **systemic absorption of a topical drop**, not through any intraocular route; the absorption pathway and the punctal-occlusion countermeasure are set out in `opmcq-c20-21`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Attribute systemic hypotension to topical beta-blockers such as timolol, and distinguish it from brimonidine's hypotension-with-apnoea risk, which the book restricts to infants.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.132 (Ch.20 Q24; its stem and options A-B print on book p.132, with options C-D and "Answer: B" opening book p.133).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most frequent complication of overuse of ocular decongestants?",
    options:["Rebound vasodilatation","Cataract formation","Glaucoma","Corneal ulcer"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Rebound vasodilatation.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.261 heads the paragraph *the most frequent complication of ocular decongestants arises from overuse*, and lists it first: **rebound vasodilation of conjunctival vessels** — *in other words, when used in excess, these preparations can increase rather than decrease the redness of the eyes.*\n\n**Why it happens.** Decongestants are **weak adrenergic-stimulating drugs** that whiten the conjunctiva by **vasoconstriction** (p.261). The three named agents are **naphazoline hydrochloride 0.012%**, **phenylephrine hydrochloride 0.12%** and **tetrahydrozoline hydrochloride 0.05%** — note that this decongestant phenylephrine is **0.12%**, an order of magnitude weaker than the 2.5% mydriatic of `opmcq-c20-27` and far weaker than the 10% strength that raises blood pressure. Squeeze the vessels down often enough and they rebound wider than they started, so the patient instils more, and the cycle closes on itself. The book's blunt verdict on the whole class: *the widespread belief that the use of these compounds is part of good ocular hygiene is a misconception* — a **cold, wet compress** to the closed lids may do as much.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Rebound vasodilatation** | ✅ Printed as the **first and most frequent** overuse complication. |\n| Cataract formation | ✗ Not attributed to decongestants anywhere in the chapter. Cataract belongs to **topical corticosteroids** (p.261 and p.270, posterior subcapsular) — a different bottle that also \"takes the redness away\". |\n| Glaucoma | ✗ ⚠️ **Not wrong, but not *most frequent*.** The very next line on p.261 says that **in rare instances, acute angle-closure glaucoma may be precipitated in susceptible eyes** by sympathomimetic drugs, because they can dilate the pupil. The stem's word *most frequent* is what separates the two options, and the book supplies the frequency for both. p.261 adds the reassurance that these drugs **may be used without harm by patients with chronic open-angle glaucoma**, because they do not raise IOP if the filtration angle is open. |\n| Corneal ulcer | ✗ Belongs to a different abused bottle — **topical anaesthetics** (p.258: toxic to the epithelium, inhibiting mitosis and cellular migration, leading to ulceration and scarring), covered in `opmcq-c20-12`. |\n\n⚠️ **The two \"self-medication\" traps of this chapter sit side by side:** the decongestant that makes the eye redder (here) and the anaesthetic that melts the cornea (`opmcq-c20-12`). Both are questions about *overuse of an over-the-counter drop*, and both distractor menus borrow from the other.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Glaucoma.)*",
    objective:"Name rebound vasodilatation as the commonest complication of decongestant overuse, and rank it against the rare precipitation of acute angle-closure glaucoma printed on the same page.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.133 (Ch.20 Q25). The bank spells it "vasodilatation"; the book prints "vasodilation" — a spelling variant, left as the bank prints it.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Which of the following drug classes is the only oral drug class utilized for long-term glaucoma management?",
    options:["Prostaglandin analogues","Alpha-2-adrenergic agonists","CAI","Beta-blockers","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**CAI — carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.267 opens the class with the sentence the stem is built from: oral CAI are **aqueous suppressants** and are **the only oral drugs utilized for long-term glaucoma management**.\n\n**The price of taking them by mouth.** The book says their use, *particularly on a chronic basis*, is limited by side effects, and lists eight:\n\n| | Oral CAI side effects (p.267) |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1 | **Numbness and tingling of extremities (paraesthesia)** |\n| 2 | Anorexia and gastrointestinal disturbances |\n| 3 | Headaches |\n| 4 | Altered taste and smell |\n| 5 | **Metabolic acidosis** |\n| 6 | **Hypokalaemia** |\n| 7 | **Predisposition to renal calculi formation** |\n| 8 | Bone marrow suppression (rare) |\n\n**Topical CAI** lower IOP by the *same mechanism* but with a **much lower — though still possible — incidence of systemic side effects**, typically only local irritation and altered taste sensation. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-825` keys paraesthesia of the extremities as the classic systemic complaint of oral acetazolamide.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Prostaglandin analogues | ✗ **Topical only.** p.266 gives them no oral form; their entire toxicity profile is ocular. |\n| Alpha-2-adrenergic agonists | ✗ **Topical only** (brimonidine drops, p.266). |\n| **CAI** | ✅ The only class the book gives an **oral** subheading, and the only one it calls oral for **long-term** management. |\n| Beta-blockers | ✗ **Topical** in ophthalmology. Systemic beta-blockers exist, but for cardiovascular indications — the book prescribes them for glaucoma only as drops (p.265), and the systemic absorption of those drops is a *problem* to be minimised (`opmcq-c20-21`), never a route of administration. |\n| None of the above | ✗ CAI is printed. |\n\n⚠️ **\"Long-term\" is the load-bearing phrase, not \"oral\".** Two other drugs in this chapter are given **by mouth** for glaucoma — the hyperosmotic agents **glycerin 50%** and **isosorbide 45%** (p.264) — but p.267 restricts their use to *acute angle closure glaucoma* (`opmcq-c20-11`), a one-off rescue measure, not maintenance. Drop \"long-term\" from the stem and the question has two defensible answers; keep it and CAI stands alone.\n\n⚠️ **Repair recorded:** the bank prints **\"longterm\"** as one word; repaired to \"long-term\" here, which is the book's own hyphenation on p.267.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Identify oral carbonic anhydrase inhibitors as the only oral class used for long-term glaucoma management, list their systemic side effects, and separate them from the orally administered hyperosmotics used acutely.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.133 (Ch.20 Q26). The stem prints "longterm" as one word; repaired here.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:"Which of the following is true regarding phenylephrine hydrochloride 2.5%?",
    options:["Its effect lasts for about 10-15 hours","It has a potent cycloplegic effect","It's often used alone as a mydriatic agent","It can cause acute hypertension and myocardial infarction","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The bank keys D**, and D is the only option that is not flatly contradicted by `ophthalmology.pdf` p.260 — but the page attributes the cardiovascular toxicity to a **different concentration** from the one in the stem. Both facts are recorded here; per project rule the printed key stands.\n\n**What p.260 says about phenylephrine 2.5%.** It is the sole mydriatic (as opposed to cycloplegic) drug in regular use, working by **stimulating the dilator pupillae muscle**:\n\n| Property (p.260) | Phenylephrine HCl **2.5%** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Onset | **30–40 minutes** |\n| Duration | **4–6 hours** |\n| Accommodation | **No cycloplegic effect** — the patient can still use near vision |\n| Used alone? | **Seldom** — mydriasis is weaker than tropicamide's and the pupil stays reactive to light |\n| Best use | **Combined with tropicamide**, whose effects are additive; the pairing is also how a **brown iris** is dilated |\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Its effect lasts for about 10-15 hours | ✗ **False.** Duration is **4–6 hours**. (10–15 h would be in cyclopentolate territory; atropine runs to **7–14 days**, p.259.) |\n| It has a potent cycloplegic effect | ✗ **False, and the reverse of the drug's defining property.** p.260 states it *does not affect accommodation*. That is precisely why it is the drug you reach for when you want a wide pupil without paralysing near vision. |\n| It's often used alone as a mydriatic agent | ✗ **False.** p.260 says it is **seldom used alone**, and gives both reasons. The option inverts a single adverb. |\n| **It can cause acute hypertension and myocardial infarction** | ✅ Keyed. ⚠️ **But p.260 attributes this to phenylephrine 10%, not 2.5%:** *phenylephrine 10% solution has been associated with acute hypertension and even with myocardial infarction in some patients*, and caution 4 says the **10%** solution should be avoided especially in **cardiac diseases, systemic hypertension or arteriosclerosis**. The 2.5% strength carries no such warning on the page. |\n| All of the above | ✗ Three of the four are false. |\n\n⚠️ **How to hold this entry.** Three concentrations of phenylephrine appear in three consecutive sections of this chapter, and the whole difficulty of the question is that the stem names one and the key describes another:\n\n| Strength | Where | Role |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **0.12%** | p.261 | Over-the-counter **decongestant** (`opmcq-c20-25`) |\n| **1.0%** | p.260 | With cyclopentolate 0.2%, the safest **infant** dilating combination |\n| **2.5%** | p.260 | The **mydriatic in regular use** — the stem's drug |\n| **10%** | p.260 | The dangerous strength: **acute hypertension, myocardial infarction**, avoid in cardiac disease |\n\nRead strictly against the book, the correct response to this stem would be *none of the above*, which the menu does not offer. Read as a single-best-answer question, D is the only option the page does not directly refute, and it is testing a real property of the **drug** even though the number is wrong. **The discrepancy is recorded here; the key is not disputed.**\n\n⚠️ There is a defensible sense in which the key is simply true: phenylephrine is a **direct alpha-1 agonist** whether instilled at 2.5% or 10%, and systemic absorption from the conjunctival sac is dose-dependent rather than all-or-none, so a hypertensive response to repeated 2.5% instillation in a susceptible patient is pharmacologically possible — *this reasoning is **not taken from the course material**; p.260 sets the threshold at 10% and prints no comment on 2.5% systemic absorption.*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"State the onset, duration and non-cycloplegic action of phenylephrine 2.5% and why it is seldom used alone, and record that the book attributes acute hypertension and myocardial infarction to the 10% solution rather than to 2.5%.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.133 (Ch.20 Q27). Key D as printed. ophthalmology.pdf p.260 attributes acute hypertension and myocardial infarction to phenylephrine 10%, not to the 2.5% named in the stem; the divergence is recorded in the explanation and the key is left as the bank prints it.' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"Pilocarpine can cause all of the following side effects except?",
    options:["Diminished vision due to pupillary dilatation","Headache","Lacrimation, salivation & perspiration","Nausea, vomiting & diarrhea","None of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Diminished vision due to pupillary *dilatation*** — the exception, because pilocarpine does the opposite. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.265 lists **diminished vision due to pupillary CONSTRICTION** among its local side effects. One word is switched, and the whole option flips.\n\n**Pilocarpine in one paragraph (p.265).** A **cholinergic-stimulating** drug available as eyedrops; it lowers IOP by **increasing aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork** — the only drug in this chapter that works on the conventional outflow route (the prostaglandins use the **uveoscleral** pathway, p.266; the beta-blockers, alpha-2 agonists and CAI all **suppress aqueous production**).\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Diminished vision due to pupillary dilatation** | ✅ **The exception.** Pilocarpine is a **miotic**. Vision does dim — but from a **small** pupil, worst in dim light and in a patient with any lens opacity, not from a large one. A cholinergic agonist stimulating the sphincter pupillae cannot dilate. |\n| Headache | ✗ Printed — *headaches from **ciliary muscle spasm***, the same accommodative spasm that causes the induced myopia and brow-ache classically described with the drug. |\n| Lacrimation, salivation & perspiration | ✗ Printed under **systemic side effects** — the textbook muscarinic triad of glandular hypersecretion. |\n| Nausea, vomiting & diarrhea | ✗ Printed under **systemic side effects** — muscarinic gut stimulation. |\n| None of the above | ✗ Option A is genuinely wrong, so an exception exists. |\n\n⚠️ **The systemic effects are rare, and the book says why:** *systemic toxicity occurs only at **5 to 10 times the usual ocular dosage***. So options C and D are real but uncommon — which is exactly what makes them convincing distractors in an *except* question, where a rare-but-printed effect and a never-printed effect look alike to anyone reading for familiarity rather than for direction.\n\n⚠️ **Mechanism as the memory hook.** Pilocarpine pulls the iris root away from the trabecular meshwork by constricting the pupil and contracting the ciliary muscle — miosis is not an incidental side effect, it *is* part of how the drug opens the angle. Any option that has pilocarpine dilating a pupil is contradicting its therapeutic mechanism, which is why this is the discriminator the chapter chose. The same reasoning is why anticholinergics, which dilate, precipitate angle closure (`opmcq-c20-29`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Reject pupillary dilatation as a pilocarpine effect by tying miosis to its trabecular outflow mechanism, and recall its printed local and systemic side effects.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.133 (Ch.20 Q28).' },

  { id:'opmcq-c20-29', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:"A 62-year-old woman with narrow anterior chamber angles develops sudden eye pain, blurred vision, and seeing halos after starting a new medication for overactive bladder. Her IOP is 48 mmHg, and the pupil is mid-dilated and nonreactive. Which class of drugs most likely triggered this attack?",
    options:["Beta-blockers","Anticholinergics","ACE inhibitors","NSAIDs"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Anticholinergics.** Every clause of the vignette is a piece of the same mechanism, and the drug class is named as a risk factor in the lecture course.\n\n| Vignette clue | What it establishes |\n| --- | --- |\n| **62-year-old woman with narrow anterior chamber angles** | The anatomical predisposition. `L12) Glaucoma 2 Cong, PACG, 2ry.txt` (\"Angle Closure Glaucoma — Patient Characteristics\" slide) lists **females**, **hyperopes**, **South-East Asians**, **advancing age** and an **affected fellow eye** as the crowded-anterior-segment risks. |\n| **Sudden eye pain, blurred vision, coloured halos** | The symptom triad of acute angle closure. L12's symptom slide: **pain ++++**, **coloured haloes around light**, **rapid diminution of vision over several hours**, redness, **nausea/vomiting (D.Dx: acute abdomen)**. |\n| **IOP 48 mmHg** | The *rapid severe rise of IOP* that defines the crisis; L12's sign slide gives **IOP ++++ (stony hard)** with corneal oedema and ciliary injection. |\n| **Mid-dilated and nonreactive pupil** | The single most specific sign. L12: **mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil**, and its risk column is headed **\"What increases the risk of angle closure glaucoma? (mid-dilated pupil)\"** — because at mid-dilatation the iris is both maximally apposed to the lens (pupillary block) and maximally bunched into the angle. |\n| **A new medication for overactive bladder** | The trigger. L12's risk column names **\"use of systemic medications (anticholinergics)\"** alongside a **dark environment** — both act by dilating the pupil into that mid-dilated position. |\n\n**Why an anticholinergic dilates.** Blocking muscarinic receptors paralyses the **sphincter pupillae**, leaving unopposed sympathetic tone on the dilator — the same pharmacology as the cycloplegics of p.259, whose systemic toxicity signature (*fever, flushing, dry skin, dry mouth, irregular rapid pulse, hallucinations, confusion, delirium*) is the anticholinergic syndrome in miniature. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.260 makes the ophthalmic warning explicit in its cautions on pupillary dilation: dilatation **can precipitate an attack of angle closure glaucoma**, and patients should be warned about **pain, headache, nausea and vomiting, halos around light and decreased vision**. p.261 repeats it for the over-the-counter decongestants — *acute angle-closure glaucoma may be precipitated in susceptible eyes by sympathomimetic drugs because they can dilate the pupil* — confirming that the operative property is **pupil dilatation**, by whichever pharmacology achieves it.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n| --- | --- |\n| Beta-blockers | ✗ **Backwards.** Topical beta-blockers *treat* glaucoma by suppressing aqueous production (p.265). They have no mydriatic action. |\n| **Anticholinergics** | ✅ The class named as a risk factor in L12 and the pharmacology behind the mydriatic cautions on p.260. |\n| ACE inhibitors | ✗ No pupillary or angle effect is attributed to them anywhere in the chapter or the lectures. |\n| NSAIDs | ✗ p.261 and p.262 give ketorolac and the topical NSAIDs an anti-inflammatory role; none dilates the pupil. |\n\n**Management, from L12's treatment slide:** hospitalisation, with systemic pressure-lowering — the **IV mannitol** and **oral hypertonic glycerol** of `opmcq-c20-11` and `opmcq-c20-14` — before definitive laser iridotomy. Grade Gain's `opqb-t20-812` runs the same emergency from the drug side.\n\n⚠️ *The specific bladder drugs (oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin) are **not taken from the course material** — neither the book chapter nor L12 names an individual antimuscarinic; both stop at the class. What the material does supply is the link that the question turns on: **systemic anticholinergics dilate the pupil, and a dilated pupil in a narrow angle closes it.***\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Pupil, EOM, Fundus.)*",
    objective:"Trace an acute angle-closure attack to a systemic anticholinergic by linking the narrow angle, the mid-dilated non-reactive pupil and the drug's mydriatic action, and reject the classes with no effect on pupil size.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.133-134 (Ch.20 Q29; the number and the first line of the stem print at the foot of book p.133, the sentence resuming at the top of book p.134 where the options and the answer line print).' }
